…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ«And as they heard these things, he added and spake a parable, because he was nigh to Jerusalem, and because they thought that the kingdom of God should immediately appear.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙnHe said therefore, A certain nobleman went into a far country to receive for himself a kingdom, and to return.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd he called his ten servants, and delivered them ten pounds, and said unto them, Occupy till I come.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙmBut his citizens hated him, and sent a message after him, saying, We will not have this man to reign over us.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙáAnd it came to pass, that when he was returned, having received the kingdom, then he commanded these servants to be called unto him, to whom he had given the money, that he might know how much every man had gained by trading.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙDThen came the first, saying, Lord, thy pound hath gained ten pounds.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And he said unto him, Well, thou good servant: because thou hast been faithful in a very little, have thou authority over ten cities.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙEAnd the second came, saying, Lord, thy pound hath gained five pounds.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ;And he said likewise to him, Be thou also over five cities.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaAnd another came, saying, Lord, behold, here is thy pound, which I have kept laid up in a napkin:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒfor I feared thee, because thou art an austere man: thou takest up that thou layedst not down, and reapest that thou didst not sow.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½And he saith unto him, Out of thine own mouth will I judge thee, thou wicked servant. Thou knewest that I was an austere man, taking up that I laid not down, and reaping that I did not sow:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙswherefore then gavest not thou my money into the bank, that at my coming I might have required mine own with usury?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd he said unto them that stood by, Take from him the pound, and give it to him that hath ten pounds.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ3(And they said unto him, Lord, he hath ten pounds.)¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFor I say unto you, That unto every one which hath shall be given; and from him that hath not, even that he hath shall be taken away from him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙmBut those mine enemies, which would not that I should reign over them, bring hither, and slay them before me.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙGAnd when he had thus spoken, he went before, ascending up to Jerusalem.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‹And it came to pass, when he was come nigh to Bethphage and Bethany, at the mount called the mount of Olives, he sent two of his disciples,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥saying, Go ye into the village over against you; in the which at your entering ye shall find a colt tied, whereon yet never man sat: loose him, and bring him hither.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkAnd if any man ask you, Why do ye loose him? thus shall ye say unto him, Because the Lord hath need of him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verse ªlast_verse$¦verses•„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPAnd they that were sent went their way, and found even as he had said unto them.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\And as they were loosing the colt, the owners thereof said unto them, Why loose ye the colt?¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ)And they said, The Lord hath need of him.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd they brought him to Jesus: and they cast their garments upon the colt, and they set Jesus thereon.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ5And as he went, they spread their clothes in the way.¦layout ªlast_verse$…¤book)§chapter«first_verse%ªlast_verse(¦verses”„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÎAnd when he was come nigh, even now at the descent of the mount of Olives, the whole multitude of the disciples began to rejoice and praise God with a loud voice for all the mighty works that they had seen;¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙksaying, Blessed be the King that cometh in the name of the Lord: peace in heaven, and glory in the highest.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_And some of the Pharisees from among the multitude said unto him, Master, rebuke thy disciples.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|And he answered and said unto them, I tell you that, if these should hold their peace, the stones would immediately cry out.¦layout ªlast_verse(…¤book)§chapter«first_verse)ªlast_verse,¦verses”„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@And when he was come near, he beheld the city, and wept over it,¦layout ªlast_verse)„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsaying, If thou hadst known, even thou, at least in this thy day, the things which belong unto thy peace! but now they are hid from thine eyes.¦layout ªlast_verse*„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFor the days shall come upon thee, that thine enemies shall cast a trench about thee, and compass thee round, and keep thee in on every side,¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ´and shall lay thee even with the ground, and thy children within thee; and they shall not leave in thee one stone upon another; because thou knewest not the time of thy visitation.¦layout ªlast_verse,…¤book)§chapter«first_verse-ªlast_verse.¦verses’„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`And he went into the temple, and began to cast out them that sold therein, and them that bought;¦layout ªlast_verse-„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgsaying unto them, It is written, My house is the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves.¦layout ªlast_verse.…¤book)§chapter«first_verse/ªlast_verse0¦verses’„¥verse/¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{And he taught daily in the temple. But the chief priests and the scribes and the chief of the people sought to destroy him,¦layout ªlast_verse/„¥verse0¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZand could not find what they might do: for all the people were very attentive to hear him.¦layout ªlast_verse0